## Type-II face-tail multiplicity and many accessible ports

Run base 99368bc1; canonical base 419ab570, Audit.142. Target:
C-II Type-II fixed-port obstruction. Exact bridge attacked: replace
the global-tail-only route estimate by a source-geometric bound on
how many actual attachment incidences can share each large face.
Dependencies: Sections 69, 73, 78, 80, 83 and 123; canonical
Audits.133--134. No conditional report is imported as a premise.

The summed non-rhombic augmented-face area is o(D^2), since every
unloaded-only rhombus retains all its internal edges in the augmented
graph. Each convex large face is within sqrt(2 area) of one line.
The nearly circular loaded hull then gives at most
(12 sqrt(rho)+o(1))D ports within rho D of any such face. Swapping
the port/face sums bounds aggregate local tails by that multiplicity
times the single global tail bank, which is at most 3 pi D.

For any fixed lambda and eta, a sufficiently small fixed rho makes
fewer than eta D actual attachment incidences fail short access
(length <lambda D) to every same-component vertex within rho D.
In the canonical macroscopic cell, uniformly extracted near-port
grids and the linear actual-attachment population yield linearly
many short-access port/grid packages. Paths and grids may overlap.
A fixed-vertex convex triangle construction also selects a proper
loaded-chord cap for every attachment of that cell, with uniformly
quadratic mass and linear attachment height.

Classification: proved candidate awaiting independent audit/promotion;
registry unreviewed, canonical=false. A worst prescribed attachment,
independent route payment, compatible finite motion and the
macroscopic inactive-container branch remain unresolved.

