## Uniform harmonic cells and full diameter-incidence coupling

Run base d63e3665; canonical base ca26397a, Audit.143. Previous goal
turn classification: progress, because its proved-candidate geometric
multiplicity and cap packages changed the next source-coupling question.
Full Level I success has not been substituted by those finite results.

The initial finite-move investigation confirmed that short contact
paths alone do not give a feasible local deformation: mixed rigidity
and positive equilibrium already obstruct nontrivial first-order
contact/diameter-feasible motions. No deformation was inferred.

New bridge: actual full-source diameter normals, unloaded degree and
the canonical pinned ledger force every actual harmonic cell to have
uniformly macroscopic diameter, attachment population and area.
An attachment with an unloaded unit neighbor forbids an angle <=30
degrees by doubling the cone's unit-circle angles. Nearby actual
diameter normals would force such an acute cell vertex if its diameter
were submacroscopic. A long thin cell would have only o(D) attachments
near at most two circle intersections, contradicting t>=delta+3.
Therefore the number of unloaded components is uniformly bounded.
This part holds in both C-II branches; it allows no unloaded component
and does not bound the source isolate population.

In Type II every cell then has uniform quadratic grain/grid mass.
Cell tiling makes the union of actual attachments approximate the
circle. A loaded-only vertex cannot have just one loaded contact,
because its positive diameter force is radial and that contact is
nearly tangent. Loaded-only chains therefore reach attachments in
uniform o(D) contact length. Diameter-direction spacing upgrades the
face-tail bank to degree-weighted incidence: all but eta D actual
diameter edges, for fixed eta and lambda, have both endpoints coupled
by <lambda D contact paths to uniform nearby actual complete grids.
This counts high-degree hubs, not just port vertices.

