## Endpoint-assignment follow-up, still exploratory

Candidate chain: gated endpoint assignment -> no marked diameter pair
internal to a nonhub branch -> actual contact-preserving region motion
with all upper/lower tests -> strict competitor. The physical-assignment
and feasible-motion arrows remain unproved, so this is exploratory.

There is a short abstract repair of Audit.138's endpoint ambiguity.
Root its dual tree at a common support hub r. For each connected
incident-region set R(p), let g(p) be its unique vertex nearest r.
If r lies in the subtree spanning R(p) union R(q), then it lies on
the path g(p)--g(q). Otherwise both gates lie in the same component
of T minus r. Connectedness and gate minimality force both entire
incident-region sets into that component, contradicting the support
hub. Thus the gated assignment has no full pair internal to a nonhub
branch. Classification: proved topological candidate awaiting audit;
this is not an unrestricted physical-source branch exclusion.

The single-region assignment in an actual owner motion is induced by
the source's translations. It cannot be replaced by gates merely to
make the diameter graph convenient. At a cut endpoint, admissibility
of the chosen translation still requires every old contact and full
source pair. Even midpoint cuts whose ends avoid all vertex marks
must be extracted as actual proper disjoint cuts and integrated with
the finite train constraints. The new topology alone does not close
that motion arrow. No source-level claim or state promotion is made.

